// chk/expr.voi — expression TYPES, judged for the first time.
//
// Everything before this file checked the shape of a program: arities, method
// sets, binding modes, names that resolve. None of it ever asked the one
// question a type checker exists to ask — does the value that arrives here
// fit the hole it is being put into. `var x int = greeting()` where
// `greeting` returns `str` compiled, ran, and put a string in an `int`.
//
// Six questions are asked here, all from one walk:
//
//   1. an INITIALISER against its annotation      `var x int = f()`
//   2. an ASSIGNMENT against the target's type    `x = f()`
//   3. an ARGUMENT against the parameter          `f(1, "a")`
//   4. a RETURNED value against the result        `return "a"`
//   5. a FIELD against the struct's declaration   `p.z`
//   6. a METHOD's existence and arity on a nominal receiver  `p.foo(1)`
//
// and, folded into 1–4, the thing that finally makes `[]Describe` mean
// something: a trait-typed hole accepts only a type that implements the
// trait, and a trait-typed RECEIVER answers only the trait's own methods.
//
// THE DESIGN RULE, and it governs every line below: THE CHECKER SAYS NOTHING
// UNLESS IT IS SURE. `ty_of` is a partial function — 0 means "I do not know"
// — and `mismatch` is written as a positive test for a KNOWN-BAD pair rather
// than as the negation of an assignability relation. Those are not the same
// program. A compatibility relation has to be complete to be safe, and any
// gap in it rejects a working program; a known-bad test has to be sound to be
// safe, and any gap in it merely loses a diagnostic. The plan's Hazards
// section records three separate occasions on which a check of the first kind
// broke a build in this repository, so this file is written the second way.
//
// WHAT IS NEVER JUDGED, and why each one is a refusal rather than an omission:
//
//   · anything whose type contains a GENERIC PARAMETER. Generics are
//     type-erased; `T` at a use site stands for a type nothing wrote down.
//     typ.HasParam is the gate — except where the receiver supplies the
//     answer, which is what typ.Subst is for (see subst_all).
//   · anything containing TK_ERROR. chk/sig.voi already reported the name;
//     a second diagnostic about the same annotation is noise.
//   · a TRAIT on the value side. A trait-typed value dispatches dynamically
//     and its dynamic type is unknown here, so it fits any hole.
//   · a std package's type. `str.Builder` resolves through typ.Resolves'
//     escape hatch precisely because it has no Voilà source; there is no
//     declaration to judge it against.
//   · a name a local binding may have SHADOWED. callsite.voi's discipline,
//     reused wholesale — the env below IS the scope, so a shadowed module
//     function simply is not found.
//   · `?T` and `T!` on either side, unwrapped one layer and then judged on
//     the payload. They are not sum types (`Some`/`Ok`/`Err` are patterns,
//     never constructors), so `?int` genuinely accepts an `int` and nil.
//
// SHADOWING AND SCOPE. `env` maps a name to a type id and is COPIED ON ENTRY
// to every nested scope, exactly as chk/bind.voi's does and for the same
// reason: a `var x str` inside a block genuinely shadows an outer `x int`.
// The copy walks map keys, whose order is nondeterministic; that is safe
// because the copy is content-only and NO DIAGNOSTIC IS EVER PRODUCED FROM A
// MAP WALK. Every loop that can reach a report goes over a declaration-
// ordered slice.
//
// ROLLOUT. Every diagnostic here is a WARNING under the code `type-mismatch`,
// not an error. The corpus sweep that justified promoting it is recorded in
// TESTING.md; see the note at the bottom of this file for what is still not
// judged and would have to be built before a promotion is honest.
package chk

use "voilac/ast"
use "voilac/tok"
use "voilac/typ"
use "std/str"

// The warning code every diagnostic in this file carries. Spelled once.
const TYPE_MISMATCH = "type-mismatch"

impl Checker {
    func check_expr_types(mut self) {
        self.collect_fields()

        let decls = self.T.Get(self.T.Kid(self.file, 1)).Kids

        // The module scope, WHOLE and first: a function body may name a
        // global declared after it.
        var menv = make(map[str]int)
        each d in decls {
            if self.T.Kind(d) == ast.GLOBAL {
                self.env_bind_stmt(self.T.Kid(d, 0), menv)
            }
        }
        each s in self.T.Get(self.T.Kid(self.file, 2)).Kids {
            self.env_bind_stmt(s, menv)
        }

        each d in decls {
            let n = self.T.Get(d)
            if n.Kind == ast.FUNC {
                self.ty_fn(d, menv, 0)
            } else if n.Kind == ast.IMPLDEC {
                // The impl's generics are in scope for every method in it,
                // and so is `self` — whose type is the impl TARGET, written
                // with its own parameters (`Stack[T]`), which is exactly what
                // makes every method of a generic type unjudgeable rather
                // than wrongly judged.
                self.Ty.PushParams(self.generic_names(self.T.Kid(d, 1)))
                let selft = self.Ty.FromAST(self.T, n.Ival)
                each m in self.T.Get(self.T.Kid(d, 0)).Kids {
                    self.ty_fn(m, menv, selft)
                }
                self.Ty.PopParams()
            } else if n.Kind == ast.EXCDEC {
                let selft = self.Ty.Nominal(n.Sval)
                each m in self.T.Get(self.T.Kid(d, 1)).Kids {
                    self.ty_fn(m, menv, selft)
                }
            } else if n.Kind == ast.TRAITDEC {
                // A trait DEFAULT body is compiled and run (low's
                // lower_trait_default), so its expressions are real code. Its
                // `self` is of no type at all — the body is shared by every
                // implementor — so it is typed with selft 0 and every check
                // that needs a receiver stays quiet.
                each m in self.T.Get(self.T.Kid(d, 0)).Kids {
                    self.ty_fn(m, menv, 0)
                }
            } else if n.Kind == ast.GLOBAL {
                // A COPY, and not menv itself. menv was built whole above
                // precisely so that a body may name a global declared after
                // it; walking each global back INTO menv re-binds the name as
                // the walk passes it, and a function declared between two
                // module-level bindings of one name was then judged against
                // the first while the runtime uses the second.
                var genv = self.copy_env(menv)
                self.ty_stmt(self.T.Kid(d, 0), genv)
            }
        }
        // The script statements ARE the module scope, so they are walked in
        // it rather than in a copy.
        self.eSelf = 0
        self.eRes = 0
        self.eFn = ""
        each s in self.T.Get(self.T.Kid(self.file, 2)).Kids {
            self.ty_stmt(s, menv)
        }
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------- the tables

    // collect_fields records every struct field's declared type node under
    // "Type|field", plus the field NAMES per type so that "has no field" can
    // be answered without a second walk. Exceptions are recorded too: their
    // fields are ordinary struct fields and cg registers them the same way.
    //
    // Declaration order (sNames, never the map) — though nothing here can
    // reach a diagnostic, the walk is ordered anyway so that a later reader
    // does not have to work out whether it may.
    func collect_fields(mut self) {
        each name in self.sNames {
            self.absorb_fields(name, self.T.Kid(self.structs[name], 0))
        }
        // … and every method NAME the program declares, from the same
        // declaration-ordered list. A trait's methods count: a default body
        // is registered for every implementor, and an abstract one is
        // somebody's obligation.
        each d in self.T.Get(self.T.Kid(self.file, 1)).Kids {
            let n = self.T.Get(d)
            var list = 0
            if n.Kind == ast.IMPLDEC or n.Kind == ast.TRAITDEC {
                list = self.T.Kid(d, 0)
            } else if n.Kind == ast.EXCDEC {
                list = self.T.Kid(d, 1)
            }
            if list == 0 { continue }
            each m in self.T.Get(list).Kids {
                if self.T.Kind(m) != ast.FUNC { continue }
                let mn = self.T.Get(m).Sval
                if mn != "" { self.mnames.add(mn) }
            }
        }
    }

    func absorb_fields(mut self, name str, list int) {
        if list == 0 { return }
        each f in self.T.Get(list).Kids {
            if self.T.Kind(f) != ast.FIELDDECL { continue }
            let fn = self.T.Get(f)
            let k = name || "|" || fn.Sval
            if k in self.flds { continue }
            self.flds[k] = f
        }
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------- functions

    // ty_fn judges one function or method body against its own signature.
    func ty_fn(mut self, fn int, outer map[str]int, selft int) {
        if self.T.Kind(fn) != ast.FUNC { return }
        let body = self.T.Kid(fn, 0)
        if body == 0 { return }
        let n = self.T.Get(fn)
        self.Ty.PushParams(self.generic_names(self.T.Kid(fn, 3)))

        var env = make(map[str]int)
        each k in keys(outer) {
            env[k] = outer[k]
        }
        each p in self.T.Get(self.T.Kid(fn, 1)).Kids {
            if self.T.Kind(p) != ast.PARAM { continue }
            let pn = self.T.Get(p)
            self.env_put(env, pn.Sval, self.param_ty(p))
        }

        let saveSelf = self.eSelf
        let saveRes = self.eRes
        let saveFn = self.eFn
        self.eSelf = selft
        self.eRes = self.single_result(fn)
        self.eFn = n.Sval
        each s in self.T.Get(body).Kids {
            self.ty_stmt(s, env)
        }
        self.eSelf = saveSelf
        self.eRes = saveRes
        self.eFn = saveFn
        self.Ty.PopParams()
    }

    // param_ty is a parameter's declared type, with the VARIADIC rule
    // applied: `args …str` binds `args` to a `[]str`, not to a `str`, and a
    // checker that missed that would accuse every use of the name.
    func param_ty(mut self, p int) int {
        let pn = self.T.Get(p)
        let base = self.Ty.FromAST(self.T, pn.Ival)
        if base == 0 { return 0 }
        if (pn.Ival2 & 16) == 0 { return base }
        return self.Ty.MkSlice(base)
    }

    // single_result is a function's result type when it declares EXACTLY one,
    // and 0 otherwise. Two results are a tuple the callers of this file are
    // not equipped to take apart, and `unit` is the empty type — chk/sig.voi's
    // result_count explains why counting the annotation is wrong.
    func single_result(mut self, fn int) int {
        let list = self.T.Kid(fn, 2)
        if self.T.NKids(list) != 1 { return 0 }
        let r = self.T.Kid(list, 0)
        let rn = self.T.Get(r)
        if rn.Kind == ast.TNAMED and rn.Sval == "unit" { return 0 }
        return self.Ty.FromAST(self.T, r)
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------- statements

    // ty_stmt walks a statement, judging what it can and binding what it
    // learns. It mirrors chk/bind.voi's bind_stmt construct for construct —
    // deliberately, because that walker's scope handling is the one that has
    // been proved right against this corpus.
    func ty_stmt(mut self, s int, env mut map[str]int) {
        if s == 0 { return }
        let n = self.T.Get(s)
        let k = n.Kind

        if k == ast.LET or k == ast.VAR {
            self.ty_exprs(self.T.Kid(s, 0), env)
            self.check_init(s, env)
            self.env_bind_stmt(s, env)
        } else if k == ast.ASSIGN {
            self.ty_exprs(self.T.Kid(s, 0), env)
            self.ty_exprs(self.T.Kid(s, 1), env)
            self.check_assign(s, env)
        } else if k == ast.RETURN {
            each a in n.Kids {
                self.ty_exprs(a, env)
            }
            self.check_return(s, env)
        } else if k == ast.BLOCK {
            self.ty_block(s, env)
        } else if k == ast.EXPRSTMT or k == ast.DROP {
            self.ty_exprs(self.T.Kid(s, 0), env)
        } else if k == ast.IF {
            self.ty_exprs(self.T.Kid(s, 0), env)
            // `if let` binds into the THEN branch only. Pattern binds carry
            // no type this release can work out, so they enter as unknown —
            // which also, and importantly, SHADOWS any outer name of the same
            // spelling instead of leaving a stale type behind.
            var inner = self.copy_env(env)
            self.env_bind_pattern(n.Ival, inner)
            self.ty_stmts_in(self.T.Kid(s, 1), inner)
            self.ty_stmt(self.T.Kid(s, 2), env)
        } else if k == ast.EACH or k == ast.FOR {
            let it = self.T.Kid(s, 0)
            self.ty_exprs(it, env)
            var inner = self.copy_env(env)
            self.bind_loop_vars(n.Sval, it, inner, k == ast.EACH)
            self.ty_stmts_in(self.T.Kid(s, 1), inner)
        } else if k == ast.WHILE or k == ast.GROUP {
            self.ty_exprs(self.T.Kid(s, 0), env)
            self.ty_block(self.T.Kid(s, 1), env)
        } else if k == ast.SWITCH {
            self.ty_exprs(self.T.Kid(s, 0), env)
            each c in self.T.Get(self.T.Kid(s, 1)).Kids {
                each v in self.T.Get(self.T.Kid(c, 0)).Kids {
                    self.ty_exprs(v, env)
                }
                var inner = self.copy_env(env)
                self.ty_stmt_list(self.T.Kid(c, 1), inner)
            }
        } else if k == ast.MATCH {
            self.ty_exprs(self.T.Kid(s, 0), env)
            each a in self.T.Get(self.T.Kid(s, 1)).Kids {
                var inner = self.copy_env(env)
                self.env_bind_pattern(self.T.Get(a).Ival, inner)
                self.ty_exprs(self.T.Kid(a, 0), inner)
                self.ty_stmt_list(self.T.Kid(a, 1), inner)
            }
        } else if k == ast.SELECTSTMT {
            each c in self.T.Get(self.T.Kid(s, 0)).Kids {
                self.ty_exprs(self.T.Kid(c, 0), env)
                self.ty_exprs(self.T.Kid(c, 1), env)
                var inner = self.copy_env(env)
                each nm in str.split(self.T.Get(c).Sval, ",") {
                    self.env_put(inner, nm, 0)
                }
                self.ty_stmt_list(self.T.Kid(c, 2), inner)
            }
        } else if k == ast.PARSESTMT {
            self.ty_exprs(self.T.Kid(s, 0), env)
            each term in self.T.Get(self.T.Kid(s, 1)).Kids {
                let tn = self.T.Get(term)
                if tn.Ival == 0 { self.env_put(env, tn.Sval, 0) }
            }
        } else if k == ast.SAY {
            each a in n.Kids {
                self.ty_exprs(a, env)
            }
        } else if k == ast.THROW {
            self.ty_exprs(self.T.Kid(s, 0), env)
            self.ty_exprs(self.T.Kid(s, 1), env)
        } else if k == ast.DEFERSTMT {
            if n.Ival2 == 1 {
                self.ty_block(self.T.Kid(s, 0), env)
            } else {
                self.ty_exprs(self.T.Kid(s, 0), env)
            }
        } else if k == ast.TRYCATCH {
            self.ty_block(self.T.Kid(s, 0), env)
            each cc in self.T.Get(self.T.Kid(s, 1)).Kids {
                var inner = self.copy_env(env)
                self.env_put(inner, self.T.Get(cc).Sval, 0)
                self.ty_block(self.T.Kid(cc, 0), inner)
            }
            self.ty_block(self.T.Kid(s, 2), env)
        }
    }

    func ty_block(mut self, b int, env map[str]int) {
        if b == 0 { return }
        var inner = self.copy_env(env)
        each s in self.T.Get(b).Kids {
            self.ty_stmt(s, inner)
        }
    }

    // ty_stmts_in walks a BLOCK whose scope has ALREADY been opened by the
    // caller (an `if let` branch, a loop body with its variables bound), so
    // it must not open a second one and lose them.
    func ty_stmts_in(mut self, b int, env mut map[str]int) {
        if b == 0 { return }
        each s in self.T.Get(b).Kids {
            self.ty_stmt(s, env)
        }
    }

    // ty_stmt_list walks a bare statement LIST — a match arm's body, a switch
    // case's — which is not a BLOCK node.
    func ty_stmt_list(mut self, list int, env mut map[str]int) {
        if list == 0 { return }
        each b in self.T.Get(list).Kids {
            self.ty_stmt(b, env)
        }
    }

    // ty_exprs types an expression for its SIDE EFFECTS — the checks that
    // hang off a call or a selection — and descends into the statements
    // hiding inside a closure or a `spawn { … }`.
    func ty_exprs(mut self, e int, env map[str]int) {
        if e == 0 { return }
        let n = self.T.Get(e)
        let k = n.Kind
        if k == ast.CLOSURE {
            var inner = self.copy_env(env)
            each p in self.T.Get(self.T.Kid(e, 1)).Kids {
                if self.T.Kind(p) != ast.PARAM { continue }
                self.env_put(inner, self.T.Get(p).Sval, self.param_ty(p))
            }
            // A closure answers to its OWN result list, not the enclosing
            // function's — chk/sig.voi refuses to judge one for exactly the
            // same reason — so the return context is cleared for its body.
            let saveRes = self.eRes
            self.eRes = 0
            each s in self.T.Get(self.T.Kid(e, 0)).Kids {
                self.ty_stmt(s, inner)
            }
            self.eRes = saveRes
            return
        }
        if k == ast.SPAWNEXPR and n.Ival2 == 1 {
            self.ty_block(self.T.Kid(e, 0), env)
            return
        }
        if k == ast.CALL {
            self.check_call(e, env)
            // The callee of `x.m(…)` is a FIELD node, and it is NOT a field
            // access: descending into it would ask check_field whether `m` is
            // a field of `x` and report the same missing method twice, once
            // in each vocabulary. Only the RECEIVER is descended into.
            let callee = self.T.Kid(e, 0)
            if self.T.Kind(callee) == ast.FIELD {
                self.ty_exprs(self.T.Kid(callee, 0), env)
            } else {
                self.ty_exprs(callee, env)
            }
            each a in self.T.Get(self.T.Kid(e, 1)).Kids {
                self.ty_exprs(self.T.Kid(a, 0), env)
            }
            return
        }
        if k == ast.FIELD {
            self.check_field(e, env)
        }
        if (k == ast.ARM or k == ast.IF) and n.Ival != 0 {
            self.ty_exprs(n.Ival, env)
        }
        each c in n.Kids {
            self.ty_exprs(c, env)
        }
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------- the checks

    // check_init judges `var x T = e` and `let x T = e`.
    //
    // Only a SINGLE-name binding is judged. `let a, b = divmod(…)` spreads a
    // tuple over two names and neither one is the type of the whole value.
    func check_init(mut self, s int, env map[str]int) {
        let n = self.T.Get(s)
        if n.Ival == 0 { return }               // no annotation, nothing to fit
        let v = self.T.Kid(s, 0)
        if v == 0 { return }                    // `var x T` — the zero value
        if n.Kind == ast.LET and len(str.split(n.Sval, ",")) != 1 { return }
        let want = self.Ty.FromAST(self.T, n.Ival)
        let got = self.ty_of(v, env)
        if not self.mismatch(want, got) { return }
        self.warn_at(v, TYPE_MISMATCH, "`{n.Sval}` is " ||
            self.tq(want) || ", but its initialiser is " || self.tq(got))
    }

    // check_assign judges `x = e` — and only a PLAIN assignment: `+=` and its
    // relatives apply an operator whose result type is the operator's
    // business, not the operand's.
    func check_assign(mut self, s int, env map[str]int) {
        let n = self.T.Get(s)
        if n.Ival != tok.ASSIGN { return }
        let lhs = self.T.Kid(s, 0)
        let rhs = self.T.Kid(s, 1)
        if lhs == 0 or rhs == 0 { return }
        if self.T.Kind(lhs) != ast.IDENT and self.T.Kind(lhs) != ast.FIELD {
            return
        }
        let want = self.ty_of(lhs, env)
        let got = self.ty_of(rhs, env)
        if not self.mismatch(want, got) { return }
        self.warn_at(rhs, TYPE_MISMATCH, "cannot assign " || self.tq(got) ||
            " to `" || self.lhs_text(lhs) || "`, which is " || self.tq(want))
    }

    // check_return judges `return e` against the enclosing function's single
    // declared result. eRes is 0 inside a closure and for every multi-result
    // signature, so both are silently skipped.
    func check_return(mut self, s int, env map[str]int) {
        if self.eRes == 0 { return }
        let n = self.T.Get(s)
        if len(n.Kids) != 1 { return }
        let v = n.Kids[0]
        let got = self.ty_of(v, env)
        if not self.mismatch(self.eRes, got) { return }
        self.warn_at(v, TYPE_MISMATCH, "`" || self.eFn || "` returns " ||
            self.tq(self.eRes) || ", but " || self.tq(got) || " is given")
    }

    // check_call judges a call's ARGUMENTS, and — when the callee is a
    // selection — the receiver's method as well.
    func check_call(mut self, call int, env map[str]int) {
        let callee = self.T.Kid(call, 0)
        if callee == 0 { return }
        let ck = self.T.Kind(callee)
        if ck == ast.IDENT {
            let nm = self.T.Get(callee).Sval
            if nm in env { return }             // shadowed by a local binding
            if not (nm in self.funcs) { return }
            let fn = self.funcs[nm]
            if self.T.NKids(self.T.Kid(fn, 3)) != 0 { return }   // generic
            self.check_args(call, self.T.Kid(fn, 1), nm, env, [], [])
        } else if ck == ast.FIELD {
            self.check_method_call(call, callee, env)
        }
    }

    // check_args pairs a call's POSITIONAL arguments with a declared
    // parameter list. A named argument or a spread makes the pairing
    // meaningless, and the whole call is abandoned — chk/callsite.voi already
    // refuses to count arity in both cases, for the same reason.
    //
    // `names`/`args` carry a generic substitution when the receiver supplied
    // one; they are empty for an ordinary function, which is never generic
    // here (check_call refuses those).
    //
    // The names are PUSHED as a generic scope as well as substituted, and
    // both steps are needed: without the push, `v T` interns as TK_ERROR —
    // `T` names no declared type — and mismatch would go silent on the very
    // parameter the substitution exists to make judgeable.
    func check_args(mut self, call int, plist int, who str,
                    env map[str]int, names []str, args []int) {
        self.Ty.PushParams(names)
        self.check_args_in(call, plist, who, env, names, args)
        self.Ty.PopParams()
    }

    func check_args_in(mut self, call int, plist int, who str,
                       env map[str]int, names []str, args []int) {
        let ps = self.T.Get(plist).Kids
        let argl = self.T.Get(self.T.Kid(call, 1)).Kids
        var i = 0
        while i < len(argl) {
            let an = self.T.Get(argl[i])
            if an.Ival == 1 { return }          // spread: unpairable
            if an.Sval != "" { return }         // named: not positional
            if i >= len(ps) { return }          // arity is callsite.voi's
            let p = ps[i]
            if self.T.Kind(p) != ast.PARAM { return }
            if (self.T.Get(p).Ival2 & 16) != 0 { return }   // variadic tail
            let want = self.subst_all(self.Ty.FromAST(self.T,
                                                      self.T.Get(p).Ival),
                                      names, args)
            let v = self.T.Kid(argl[i], 0)
            let got = self.ty_of(v, env)
            if self.mismatch(want, got) {
                self.warn_at(v, TYPE_MISMATCH, "argument " || str(i + 1) ||
                    " of `" || who || "` is " || self.tq(want) || ", but " ||
                    self.tq(got) || " is given")
            }
            i += 1
        }
    }

    // check_method_call is the hole the task list called the biggest one
    // left: `x.m(…)` is resolved entirely at run time, by string, against a
    // flat table — and `vl_callm` ABORTS when it finds nothing.
    //
    // Judged only when the receiver's type is a nominal this program declares
    // in full. A primitive receiver is not judged (`s.upper()` is
    // `str.upper(s)` through the method-call duality, and the duality set has
    // no rows in the method table); nor is a std package's type, nor a box, a
    // slice, a map — all of which answer methods the runtime provides and no
    // declaration lists.
    func check_method_call(mut self, call int, callee int, env map[str]int) {
        let mn = self.T.Get(callee).Sval
        let recv = self.T.Kid(callee, 0)
        let rt = self.ty_of(recv, env)
        if rt == 0 { return }
        let t = self.Ty.Get(rt)
        if t.Kind != typ.TK_NAMED { return }
        let tn = t.Name

        // A TRAIT-typed receiver. The obvious rule — "only the trait's own
        // methods" — IS WRONG, and it was written that way first and caught
        // by review against a program that runs:
        //
        //     var d Describe = Point{x: 1}
        //     say d.extra()          // an inherent method of Point
        //
        // vl_callm resolves by the value's DYNAMIC type name (call.c's
        // vl_find_method), and the static annotation plays no part whatever.
        // So every method of the concrete type is reachable through a
        // trait-typed binding, and this checker does not know the concrete
        // type.
        //
        // What is still a fact is that a name NO declaration in the whole
        // program spells cannot be dispatched by anything — the flat table
        // has no row to find and vl_callm aborts. That is the only thing
        // said here.
        //
        // The trait's OWN methods keep their arity and argument checks: the
        // implementation's shape is judged against the trait's by
        // chk/traits.voi, so the trait declaration speaks for every
        // implementor.
        if tn in self.traits {
            let want = self.trait_method(tn, mn)
            if want == 0 {
                if mn in self.mnames { return }
                if mn == "str" or mn == "clone" { return }
                self.warn_at(callee, TYPE_MISMATCH, "no method `" || mn ||
                    "` is declared anywhere in this program, and `" || tn ||
                    "` is a trait — a trait-typed value dispatches on its " ||
                    "runtime type (§3.6)")
                return
            }
            self.method_arity(call, want, tn, mn)
            self.check_args(call, self.T.Kid(want, 1), tn || "." || mn, env,
                            [], [])
            return
        }

        if not (tn in self.structs) { return }
        let k = tn || "|" || mn
        if not (k in self.mset) {
            // A field holding a callable is dispatched too (call.c step 4),
            // and `str`/`clone` are answered for every value whatsoever
            // (step 2). Neither leaves a row anywhere a checker can walk.
            if k in self.flds { return }
            if mn == "str" or mn == "clone" { return }
            self.warn_at(callee, TYPE_MISMATCH, "`" || tn ||
                "` has no method `" || mn || "` (§3.6)")
            return
        }
        let m = self.mset[k]
        self.method_arity(call, m, tn, mn)
        let names = self.impl_params(tn, mn)
        self.check_args(call, self.T.Kid(m, 1), tn || "." || mn, env,
                        names, t.Args)
    }

    // method_arity is chk/callsite.voi's arity check, which only ever saw
    // plain function calls: a method's parameter list is reached through the
    // method table and nothing counted it.
    func method_arity(mut self, call int, m int, tn str, mn str) {
        var given = 0
        each a in self.T.Get(self.T.Kid(call, 1)).Kids {
            let an = self.T.Get(a)
            if an.Ival == 1 { return }
            if an.Sval != "" { return }
            given += 1
        }
        var min = 0
        var max = 0
        each p in self.T.Get(self.T.Kid(m, 1)).Kids {
            let pn = self.T.Get(p)
            if (pn.Ival2 & 16) != 0 { return }      // variadic: uncountable
            max += 1
            if self.T.Kid(p, 0) == 0 { min += 1 }
        }
        if given >= min and given <= max { return }
        var wants = str(max)
        if min != max { wants = str(min) || " to " || str(max) }
        self.warn_at(call, TYPE_MISMATCH, "`" || tn || "." || mn ||
            "` takes " || wants || plural(max, " argument", " arguments") ||
            ", but " || str(given) || plural(given, " is", " are") ||
            " given")
    }

    // check_field judges `x.f` where `f` is not being called. A struct has
    // exactly the fields it declares.
    //
    // A name the method table holds is nevertheless EXEMPT, and the reason is
    // not that a bare method name is a method value — it is not: vl_field
    // (runtime/src/types.c) has no method fallback and `say p.norm` aborts.
    // The exemption stands because this walker sees `p.norm` in contexts the
    // lowerer may yet rewrite, and losing a diagnostic on a spelling that
    // names something real is the safe direction. The two universals go the
    // same way.
    func check_field(mut self, e int, env map[str]int) {
        let fname = self.T.Get(e).Sval
        let rt = self.ty_of(self.T.Kid(e, 0), env)
        if rt == 0 { return }
        let t = self.Ty.Get(rt)
        if t.Kind != typ.TK_NAMED { return }
        let tn = t.Name
        if not (tn in self.structs) { return }
        let k = tn || "|" || fname
        if k in self.flds { return }
        if k in self.mset { return }
        if fname == "str" or fname == "clone" { return }
        self.warn_at(e, TYPE_MISMATCH, "`" || tn || "` has no field `" ||
            fname || "` (§3.4)")
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------ typing

    // ty_of is the inference, and it is PARTIAL BY DESIGN: 0 means "no idea",
    // and every caller treats 0 as "say nothing". Only the forms whose type
    // is written down somewhere — a literal, an annotated binding, a
    // declared signature, a composite literal — produce an answer.
    func ty_of(mut self, e int, env map[str]int) int {
        if e == 0 { return 0 }
        let n = self.T.Get(e)
        let k = n.Kind

        if k == ast.INT   { return self.Ty.Nominal("int") }
        if k == ast.FLOAT { return self.Ty.Nominal("float") }
        if k == ast.DEC   { return self.Ty.Nominal("dec") }
        if k == ast.RUNE  { return self.Ty.Nominal("rune") }
        if k == ast.BOOL  { return self.Ty.Nominal("bool") }
        if k == ast.STR or k == ast.INTERP { return self.Ty.Nominal("str") }
        if k == ast.NILLIT { return 0 }
        if k == ast.SELF  { return self.eSelf }
        if k == ast.IDENT {
            if n.Sval in env { return env[n.Sval] }
            return 0
        }
        if k == ast.COMPOSITE { return self.Ty.FromAST(self.T, n.Ival) }
        if k == ast.UNARY {
            if n.Ival == tok.NOT { return self.Ty.Nominal("bool") }
            return 0
        }
        if k == ast.BINARY {
            // COMPARISONS and the logical operators are the only ones whose
            // result type does not depend on the operands' — `a || b` is
            // concatenation over strings AND slices, and `a + b` widens
            // across the numeric lattice (§3.2). Answering 0 for the rest
            // costs diagnostics and invents none.
            if is_bool_op(n.Ival) { return self.Ty.Nominal("bool") }
            return 0
        }
        if k == ast.FIELD  { return self.field_ty(e, env) }
        if k == ast.CALL   { return self.call_ty(e, env) }
        if k == ast.MUSTEXPR or k == ast.TRYEXPR {
            // Both unwrap one `?T`/`T!` layer.
            return self.unwrap(self.ty_of(self.T.Kid(e, 0), env))
        }
        return 0
    }

    // field_ty is the type of `x.f` when the receiver is a struct this
    // program declares. The receiver's own type ARGUMENTS are substituted
    // into the field's declared type, so `Stack[int]`'s `items []T` is
    // `[]int` and not the unjudgeable `[]T`.
    func field_ty(mut self, e int, env map[str]int) int {
        let rt = self.ty_of(self.T.Kid(e, 0), env)
        if rt == 0 { return 0 }
        let t = self.Ty.Get(rt)
        if t.Kind != typ.TK_NAMED { return 0 }
        let k = t.Name || "|" || self.T.Get(e).Sval
        if not (k in self.flds) { return 0 }
        self.Ty.PushParams(self.decl_params(t.Name))
        let ft = self.Ty.FromAST(self.T, self.T.Get(self.flds[k]).Ival)
        self.Ty.PopParams()
        return self.subst_all(ft, self.decl_params(t.Name), t.Args)
    }

    // call_ty is the type of a call's value: a declared function's single
    // result, a method's single result, or a primitive CONVERSION (`int(x)`,
    // `str(x)`), which is the one native whose result type is its own name.
    func call_ty(mut self, e int, env map[str]int) int {
        let callee = self.T.Kid(e, 0)
        if callee == 0 { return 0 }
        let ck = self.T.Kind(callee)
        if ck == ast.IDENT {
            let nm = self.T.Get(callee).Sval
            if nm in env { return 0 }               // shadowed
            if nm in self.funcs {
                let fn = self.funcs[nm]
                if self.T.NKids(self.T.Kid(fn, 3)) != 0 { return 0 }
                return self.single_result(fn)
            }
            // A conversion, but only when nothing else claims the spelling:
            // `str(x)` is the conversion, while a user function called `str`
            // was already answered above and an enum VARIANT of that name
            // must not be read as one.
            if typ.IsPrimitive(nm) and not (nm in self.natShadow) {
                return self.Ty.Nominal(nm)
            }
            return 0
        }
        if ck != ast.FIELD { return 0 }
        let mn = self.T.Get(callee).Sval
        let rt = self.ty_of(self.T.Kid(callee, 0), env)
        if rt == 0 { return 0 }
        let t = self.Ty.Get(rt)
        if t.Kind != typ.TK_NAMED { return 0 }
        if t.Name in self.traits {
            let tm = self.trait_method(t.Name, mn)
            if tm == 0 { return 0 }
            return self.single_result(tm)
        }
        let k = t.Name || "|" || mn
        if not (k in self.mset) { return 0 }
        let m = self.mset[k]
        let names = self.impl_params(t.Name, mn)
        self.Ty.PushParams(names)
        let r = self.single_result(m)
        self.Ty.PopParams()
        return self.subst_all(r, names, t.Args)
    }

    // unwrap peels one `?T` or `T!` layer, which is what `must`, `try` and a
    // successful `else` all do. Anything else is returned untouched.
    func unwrap(mut self, id int) int {
        if id == 0 { return 0 }
        let t = self.Ty.Get(id)
        if t.Kind == typ.TK_OPTION or t.Kind == typ.TK_RESULT { return t.Elem }
        return id
    }

    // --------------------------------------------------------- generics

    // decl_params names the generic parameters of the declaration a nominal
    // type denotes — `Stack`'s `[T]`.
    func decl_params(self, name str) []str {
        if name in self.structs {
            return self.generic_names(self.T.Kid(self.structs[name], 1))
        }
        if name in self.enums {
            return self.generic_names(self.T.Kid(self.enums[name], 1))
        }
        return []
    }

    // impl_params names the generic parameters that a METHOD's types are
    // written in terms of, expressed as the TARGET's positional parameters.
    //
    // The distinction matters and is not pedantry: `impl[E] Stack[E]` writes
    // its methods in `E` while the struct is declared `Stack[T]`, and a
    // receiver typed `Stack[int]` supplies its argument POSITIONALLY. So the
    // list returned here is read off the impl block's own TARGET annotation —
    // position i of `Stack[E]` is `E` — which is exactly what pairs with
    // position i of the receiver's `Stack[int]`.
    //
    // Empty when the method is not found in an impl block at all (an
    // exception's own method, a trait default), which makes subst_all a
    // no-op and leaves any `T` in the signature unjudgeable. That is the
    // right answer: there is no receiver argument to put in its place.
    func impl_params(mut self, tn str, mn str) []str {
        let k = tn || "|" || mn
        if k in self.implOf {
            let d = self.implOf[k]
            let te = self.T.Get(d).Ival
            var out = []
            if te == 0 or self.T.Kind(te) != ast.TNAMED { return out }
            let mine = self.generic_names(self.T.Kid(d, 1))
            each a in self.T.Get(te).Kids {
                var nm = ""
                if self.T.Kind(a) == ast.TNAMED {
                    let cand = self.T.Get(a).Sval
                    each g in mine {
                        if g == cand { nm = cand }
                    }
                }
                out.append(nm)
            }
            return out
        }
        return []
    }

    // subst_all replaces each named parameter by the receiver's type argument
    // at the same position. A name that is "" (a target position written with
    // a concrete type, as in `impl Stack[int]`) substitutes nothing.
    func subst_all(mut self, id int, names []str, args []int) int {
        if id == 0 { return 0 }
        var out = id
        var i = 0
        while i < len(names) and i < len(args) {
            if names[i] != "" and args[i] != 0 {
                out = self.Ty.Subst(out, names[i], args[i])
            }
            i += 1
        }
        return out
    }

    // trait_method finds a trait's declaration of `mn`, or 0.
    func trait_method(self, tn str, mn str) int {
        if not (tn in self.traits) { return 0 }
        each m in self.T.Get(self.T.Kid(self.traits[tn], 0)).Kids {
            if self.T.Kind(m) != ast.FUNC { continue }
            if self.T.Get(m).Sval == mn { return m }
        }
        return 0
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------- the verdict

    // mismatch is the ONE place a type is judged wrong, and it is a positive
    // test for a KNOWN-BAD pair — never the negation of a compatibility
    // relation. See this file's header for why the difference is the whole
    // safety argument.
    //
    // It answers true only when both sides are SOLID: a concrete nominal
    // type, or a slice/map/set of one, with no unknown, no error, no generic
    // parameter and no trait anywhere inside. Everything else is false, which
    // is to say silent.
    func mismatch(mut self, want int, got int) bool {
        if want == 0 or got == 0 { return false }
        if self.Ty.HasError(want) or self.Ty.HasError(got) { return false }
        if self.Ty.HasParam(want) or self.Ty.HasParam(got) { return false }
        if want == got { return false }

        let w = self.Ty.Get(want)
        let g = self.Ty.Get(got)

        // A borrow is the same value, lent. `&T` and `T` fit each other's
        // holes at every site this file checks (§6.1 governs WRITING through
        // one, which is chk/bind.voi's business).
        if w.Kind == typ.TK_BORROW { return self.mismatch(w.Elem, got) }
        if g.Kind == typ.TK_BORROW { return self.mismatch(want, g.Elem) }

        // `?T` accepts a T and accepts nil; `T!` accepts a T and accepts a
        // thrown error. Neither is a sum type with constructors, so the
        // payload is the only thing there is to compare.
        if w.Kind == typ.TK_OPTION or w.Kind == typ.TK_RESULT {
            return self.mismatch(w.Elem, got)
        }
        // The other direction is NOT symmetric — putting a `?int` where an
        // `int` is wanted is a real error — but recognising it needs the
        // nil-exclusivity analysis that is still deferred, and `try`/`must`/
        // `else` are already unwrapped by ty_of. Silent.
        if g.Kind == typ.TK_OPTION or g.Kind == typ.TK_RESULT { return false }

        // A box is a handle, not the value: `shared[T]` answers `T`'s methods
        // through the runtime and is written where a `T` is wanted all over
        // the corpus.
        if w.Kind == typ.TK_BOX or g.Kind == typ.TK_BOX { return false }

        if w.Kind == typ.TK_SLICE and g.Kind == typ.TK_SLICE {
            // Extents are not compared: `[3]int` and `[]int` are the same
            // element type and the lowerer treats them alike.
            return self.mismatch(w.Elem, g.Elem)
        }
        if (w.Kind == typ.TK_SET and g.Kind == typ.TK_SET) or
           (w.Kind == typ.TK_CHAN and g.Kind == typ.TK_CHAN) {
            return self.mismatch(w.Elem, g.Elem)
        }
        if w.Kind == typ.TK_MAP and g.Kind == typ.TK_MAP {
            if self.mismatch(w.Key, g.Key) { return true }
            return self.mismatch(w.Elem, g.Elem)
        }

        if w.Kind != typ.TK_NAMED or g.Kind != typ.TK_NAMED {
            // A structural type against something else — a function type, a
            // tuple, a slice where a nominal is wanted. Every one of those IS
            // a real mismatch, and not one of them can be produced by ty_of
            // with enough confidence to say so. Silent.
            return false
        }
        return self.nominal_mismatch(w.Name, g.Name)
    }

    // nominal_mismatch judges two names. Both must be SOLID — a primitive or
    // a type this program declares whole — before a difference between them
    // is a fact rather than an admission of ignorance.
    func nominal_mismatch(mut self, wn str, gn str) bool {
        if wn == gn { return false }
        // `unit` is the empty type: it is what a function with no result
        // "returns", and nothing is wrong with any value flowing into it.
        if wn == "unit" or gn == "unit" { return false }
        // The numeric lattice (§3.2) widens across the integer and float
        // families and the literals are untyped, so any numeric fits any
        // numeric hole here. Narrowing is chk/checks.voi's business and it
        // judges the LITERAL, which is where the information is.
        if is_numeric(wn) and is_numeric(gn) { return false }

        // A trait-typed hole takes any implementor, and the answer is a fact
        // only when this program can see the whole method set — judgeable is
        // chk/traits.voi's, unchanged and deliberately not re-derived.
        if wn in self.traits {
            if not self.judgeable(gn) { return false }
            return self.missing_for(gn, wn) != ""
        }
        // A trait on the VALUE side dispatches dynamically: its runtime type
        // is not written anywhere and may well be the one wanted.
        if gn in self.traits { return false }

        if not self.solid(wn) or not self.solid(gn) { return false }
        return true
    }

    // solid reports whether a nominal name is one this checker knows the
    // whole story about. A primitive, a struct, an enum or a `type`
    // declaration qualifies. An EXCEPTION does not: `error` and the
    // predeclared exceptions are related by a subtyping the checker does not
    // model, and `throws IOError` catching an `error` is ordinary code. Nor
    // does a std package's type, which typ.Resolves waves through precisely
    // because it has no declaration to compare against.
    func solid(self, name str) bool {
        if typ.IsPrimitive(name) { return true }
        if name in self.excs { return false }
        if name in self.structs { return true }
        if name in self.enums { return true }
        return false
    }

    // tq renders a type for a diagnostic, in backticks.
    func tq(self, id int) str {
        return "`" || self.Ty.Text(id) || "`"
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------ the env

    func copy_env(self, env map[str]int) map[str]int {
        var out = make(map[str]int)
        each k in keys(env) {
            out[k] = env[k]
        }
        return out
    }

    // env_put records a name's type, ignoring the ones that are not bindings:
    // the blank `_`, and the empty half of a "bind,bindok" pair. A name whose
    // type is unknown is entered ANYWAY, with 0 — that is what makes an inner
    // binding SHADOW an outer one instead of leaving its type behind.
    func env_put(self, env mut map[str]int, name str, id int) {
        if name == "" or name == "_" { return }
        env[name] = id
    }

    // env_bind_stmt enters the names a LET or VAR introduces, with the type
    // its annotation gives or the one its value can be inferred to have.
    func env_bind_stmt(mut self, s int, env mut map[str]int) {
        if s == 0 { return }
        let n = self.T.Get(s)
        if n.Kind != ast.LET and n.Kind != ast.VAR { return }
        var id = 0
        if n.Ival != 0 {
            id = self.Ty.FromAST(self.T, n.Ival)
        } else {
            id = self.ty_of(self.T.Kid(s, 0), env)
        }
        if n.Kind == ast.VAR {
            self.env_put(env, n.Sval, id)
            return
        }
        let names = str.split(n.Sval, ",")
        if len(names) != 1 {
            // A destructuring `let`: the value is a tuple and no name has its
            // type. They are entered as unknown so that they still SHADOW.
            each nm in names {
                self.env_put(env, nm, 0)
            }
            return
        }
        self.env_put(env, names[0], id)
    }

    // env_bind_pattern enters a pattern's binds as unknown. A variant's
    // payload type is decidable — it is a VARIANTDEC's field list — but the
    // enum the pattern belongs to is not known without the subject's type,
    // and guessing it from the global variant namespace would be exactly the
    // kind of confident wrong answer this file refuses to give.
    func env_bind_pattern(mut self, p int, env mut map[str]int) {
        if p == 0 { return }
        let n = self.T.Get(p)
        if n.Kind == ast.PBIND {
            self.env_put(env, n.Sval, 0)
            return
        }
        each sub in n.Kids {
            self.env_bind_pattern(sub, env)
        }
    }

    // bind_loop_vars enters a loop's variables, and the SHAPE of an `each`
    // is per-iterable rather than uniform — this was checked against the
    // runtime rather than assumed, because guessing it wrong is a false
    // positive on every use of the variable:
    //
    //     each x in xs        a slice yields ELEMENTS
    //     each i, x in xs     … and an index alongside them
    //     each v in m         a map yields VALUES, not keys
    //     each k, v in m      … and the key alongside them
    //     each e in s         a set yields its elements
    //
    // Anything else — a range, a channel, a reader, a string, a struct with
    // `next()` — binds unknown. Binding a name to 0 still SHADOWS an outer
    // name of the same spelling, which is the point of doing it at all.
    func bind_loop_vars(mut self, sval str, iter int, env mut map[str]int,
                        is_each bool) {
        // Sval is "a,b|label" and the parser writes the comma ALWAYS, so a
        // one-variable `each` arrives as "q," — the empty half has to be
        // dropped before the count means anything. Reading it as two names
        // bound `q` to the INDEX of a slice instead of its element, and every
        // use of `q` was then reported against the wrong type. (chk/bind.voi
        // and chk/callsite.voi both split the same string and are unaffected:
        // they enter every half into a set, and "" is ignored on the way in.)
        var names = []
        each part in str.split(str.split(sval, "|")[0], ",") {
            if part != "" { names.append(part) }
        }
        var et = 0      // the value bound by the LAST name
        var ft = 0      // the index or key bound by the first of two
        if is_each and iter != 0 {
            let it = self.ty_of(iter, env)
            if it != 0 {
                let t = self.Ty.Get(it)
                if t.Kind == typ.TK_SLICE {
                    et = t.Elem
                    ft = self.Ty.Nominal("int")
                } else if t.Kind == typ.TK_MAP {
                    et = t.Elem
                    ft = t.Key
                } else if t.Kind == typ.TK_SET or t.Kind == typ.TK_CHAN {
                    et = t.Elem
                }
            }
        }
        if len(names) == 1 {
            self.env_put(env, names[0], et)
            return
        }
        var i = 0
        while i < len(names) {
            var id = 0
            if i == 0 { id = ft }
            if i == len(names) - 1 { id = et }
            self.env_put(env, names[i], id)
            i += 1
        }
    }

}

// is_bool_op reports whether a binary operator's result is a `bool`
// regardless of its operands.
func is_bool_op(op int) bool {
    return op == tok.LT or op == tok.LE or op == tok.GT or op == tok.GE or
           op == tok.EQ or op == tok.NE or op == tok.AND or op == tok.OR or
           op == tok.IN
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IS STILL NOT JUDGED, and what a promotion to a hard error would need.
//
// The sweep behind this file is recorded in TESTING.md: 156 programs, one
// report, and that one is a negative fixture whose program genuinely cannot
// run. The code is a WARNING and DEFAULTS OFF all the same, and the reason is
// not the measurement — it is that everything below is a place where the
// inference is silent rather than right, and a check that has never been
// wrong in a corpus it was written against has not yet been tested.
//
//   · `?T` NIL-EXCLUSIVITY. `var x int = maybe()` where maybe returns `?int`
//     is a real error and is not reported: mismatch unwraps the option on the
//     VALUE side and says nothing. Reporting it needs to know where a `?T`
//     has been narrowed — `if x != nil`, `match`, `else`, `must` — and a
//     version without that narrowing would reject the ordinary way of using
//     an option.
//   · MULTIPLE RESULTS. A function declaring two results is skipped entirely
//     (single_result answers 0), so `let a, b = f()` types neither name and
//     `return f()` forwarding a tuple is not judged. It needs a tuple type
//     flowing through the destructuring, which nothing here builds.
//   · ENUM VARIANT PAYLOADS. A pattern's binds enter as unknown. The payload
//     types are written down — a VARIANTDEC's field list — but the enum they
//     belong to is only knowable from the subject's type, and the variant
//     namespace is global, so guessing from the name would be a confident
//     wrong answer.
//   · ENUM and EXCEPTION receivers. Method existence is judged for STRUCTS
//     only. An exception answers `message` and `trace` from err_method with
//     no row anywhere, and the subtyping between `error` and the predeclared
//     exceptions is not modelled (see solid).
//   · COLLECTION LITERALS. `[]`, `[1, 2]`, `make(map[…])` and `set([…])` all
//     type as unknown; only an annotation gives a collection a type here.
//   · OPERATORS. Only the comparisons and the logical operators are typed.
//     `a || b` concatenates strings AND slices and `a + b` widens across the
//     numeric lattice, and neither is worth guessing at.
//   · CLOSURE and FUNCTION-TYPED values. A `fn(int) int` in a hole expecting
//     something else is a real mismatch that mismatch declines to make (see
//     its last arm), because ty_of never produces a TK_FN to compare.
//
// A promotion to SEV_ERROR wants two things this release does not have: a
// release's worth of field exposure with the warning on, and the option
// narrowing above — because the first thing anyone will do with `--strict` is
// write `?T` code, and being silent there is far better than being wrong.
